{"term":"حلمة الثدي","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ḥalamat al-thady","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"nipple","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":15,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical compound for the nipple: ḥalama (nipple) combined with al-thady (of the breast).","english_term":"nipple","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Compound clinical phrase; ḥalama alone is also widely used as the standalone word for nipple in both clinical and everyday speech."}
{"term":"الغدة الثديية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-ghudda al-thadyiyya","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"breast","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":97,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":42,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical medical term for the mammary gland; used in anatomical, pathological, and oncological contexts.","english_term":"mammary gland","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Highly technical; rarely encountered outside medical literature or formal health education; ghudda means 'gland' and thadyiyya is the adjectival form of thady ('breast')."}
{"term":"هالة الحلمة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"hālat al-ḥalama","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"areola","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":35,"recognition_pct_peak":35,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical compound for the areola: hāla (halo or ring) combined with al-ḥalama (of the nipple).","english_term":"areola","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Rarely used outside clinical or lactation-medicine contexts; hāla captures the ring or halo shape accurately."}
{"term":"بزّ","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"bazz","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude colloquial slang for a single female breast; the plural form bizāz is more common in actual usage.","english_term":"tit","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Primarily dialectal ʿāmmiyya rather than true MSA; very widespread across Levantine and Egyptian dialects; considered highly offensive in polite company."}
{"term":"بزاز","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"bizāz","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude colloquial plural for female breasts; the most common vulgar term for breasts across Arabic-speaking populations.","english_term":"tits","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The plural of bazz; far more common than the singular in actual slang usage; appears prominently in crude song lyrics, stand-up comedy, and online discourse across the Arab world."}
{"term":"ضرع","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ḍarʿ","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Primary meaning: animal udder or teat; applied crudely to human female breasts as a dehumanizing or hypersexualized descriptor.","english_term":"udder","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Comparing a woman's breasts to an animal's udder is among the most dehumanizing framings available in Arabic crude speech; often implies large, pendulous breasts; the animal register amplifies the insult."}
{"term":"كرة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"kura","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":40,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'ball'; used informally to describe round, full breasts; the dual form kuratayn is more explicit in this sense.","english_term":"ball","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is 'ball' (sports/sphere); the breast sense requires context; the dual kuratayn ('two balls') makes the sexual reference unambiguous; mild compared to bazz/bizāz."}
{"term":"نتوء","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"natūʾ","category":"organs","subcategory":"breast","concept":"nipple","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":30,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'protrusion' or 'prominence'; used euphemistically or descriptively for a nipple, especially a visibly erect one.","english_term":"protrusion","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Not a dedicated anatomical term; used contextually to describe the nipple as a physical protrusion rather than as a named structure; implies visibility or prominence."}
{"term":"أليتان","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"alyatān","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":63,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical and literary dual form meaning 'the two buttocks'; singular alya refers to one buttock.","english_term":"buttocks","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Proper MSA dual; more commonly encountered in formal medical or literary Arabic than in everyday speech; the dual form reflects that there are two gluteal masses."}
{"term":"مؤخرة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"muʾakhkhira","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":15,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'the rear' or 'the posterior'; the most common polite-to-neutral MSA term for the buttocks.","english_term":"rear end","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Directional euphemism derived from ʾakhkhara ('to put behind/delay'); functions as the polite default across all registers and age groups; extremely high recognition pan-Arab."}
{"term":"عجيزة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ʿajīza","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":20,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Arabic term for the buttocks or posterior; used in literary, poetic, and some dialectal contexts.","english_term":"buttocks","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Has a distinctly classical and poetic register; semantically unrelated to ʿajūz ('old woman') despite surface similarity; common in classical Arabic love and erotic poetry."}
{"term":"ردف","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ridf","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":55,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Classical Arabic term for a single buttock or the buttocks in general; used in literary and poetic descriptions of the body.","english_term":"buttock","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Singular; more formal and literary than colloquial; the dual ridafān is common in both classical poetry and colloquial erotic speech; appears extensively in classical Arabic erotic literature."}
{"term":"إليتان","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ilyatān","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":72,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical dual form for the buttocks; orthographic variant of alyatān with the hamza shifted from the ʾalif to the yāʾ.","english_term":"buttocks","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Variant spelling of alyatān; both forms appear in medical literature; slightly lower recognition because the variant spelling is less standardized."}
{"term":"طيز","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ṭīz","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The most widespread and vulgar colloquial Arabic term for the buttocks; used as a general expletive and insult as well.","english_term":"ass","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Polysemous: can refer to the buttocks as a whole or specifically the anus depending on context (see also anus section); among the most taboo single words in Arabic; recognition is near-universal across all dialects."}
{"term":"ردفان","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ridafān","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dual form of ridf ('buttock'); refers to the two buttocks together; used in both classical poetry and crude colloquial speech.","english_term":"buttocks","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The dual form of ردف; classically literary in origin but frequently deployed in crude colloquial contexts; the crude register is context-dependent rather than inherent to the word itself."}
{"term":"وراء","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"warāʾ","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":8,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'behind' or 'rear'; used as a mild spatial euphemism for the buttocks in polite or children's speech.","english_term":"behind","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Primary meaning is the preposition or adverb meaning 'behind/after'; the buttocks sense is a minor contextual euphemism rather than a dedicated anatomical term; essentially equivalent to English 'behind' as a euphemism."}
{"term":"خلف","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"khalf","category":"organs","subcategory":"buttocks","concept":"buttocks","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":8,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'back' or 'behind'; used as a mild directional euphemism for the posterior or buttocks.","english_term":"back","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Like warāʾ, primary meaning is directional; خلف is also a common male given name in the Arab world, which can create unintentional double entendre in crude speech."}
{"term":"شرج","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"sharj","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":30,"technical":82,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard MSA clinical term for the anus; used in medical, anatomical, and formal health education contexts.","english_term":"anus","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The canonical MSA term; still considered taboo in polite discourse despite its clinical register; the derived adjective sharjī ('anal') is used in medical compound terms throughout Arabic medical literature."}
{"term":"فتحة الشرج","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"fatḥat al-sharj","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":18,"technical":88,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical compound meaning 'the anal opening'; fatḥa means 'opening' or 'orifice'; used in surgical and gastroenterological contexts.","english_term":"anal opening","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Descriptive clinical construct-state compound; commonly encountered in colorectal surgery and gastroenterology literature; lower recognition than sharj alone due to the compound structure."}
{"term":"العضلة العاصرة الشرجية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-ʿaḍala al-ʿāṣira al-sharjiyya","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"sphincter","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":3,"technical":99,"recognition_pct_current":22,"recognition_pct_peak":22,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical phrase for the anal sphincter: al-ʿaḍala (the muscle) + al-ʿāṣira (the constricting/squeezing) + al-sharjiyya (anal/rectal).","english_term":"anal sphincter","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Highly technical medical terminology known primarily to healthcare professionals; ʿāṣira accurately captures the constricting function from the root ʿ-ṣ-r ('to squeeze/press'); not encountered in lay discourse."}
{"term":"الفتحة الشرجية","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"al-fatḥa al-sharjiyya","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":18,"technical":87,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical compound for the anal orifice using the adjectival form; near-synonym of fatḥat al-sharj with different morphological construction.","english_term":"anal orifice","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Uses the adjectival relative clause sharjiyya ('anal/rectal') rather than the construct state; both forms are acceptable in clinical usage; the adjectival form is slightly more formal."}
{"term":"است","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ist","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":92,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Highly crude colloquial term for the anus or buttocks; also functions as a general expletive and insult equivalent to 'asshole'.","english_term":"asshole","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Among the most taboo single words in Arabic; etymologically an ancient Semitic root attested across several languages; particularly prominent in Egyptian and Levantine dialects but widely understood across the Arab world; used as an insult for contemptible people."}
{"term":"طيز","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ṭīz","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":93,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude colloquial term referring to both the buttocks and the anus; in explicit sexual contexts the anus reading is foregrounded.","english_term":"ass","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Polysemous: the buttocks reading dominates everyday use; the anus reading emerges in explicit or clinical-crude contexts; see also entry in the buttocks section; near-universal recognition pan-Arab."}
{"term":"حلق","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ḥalq","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Primary meaning: throat or neck opening; used as crude slang for the anus in several Arabic dialects, apparently by analogy with a circular muscular orifice.","english_term":"asshole","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The throat-to-anus transfer is most common in Egyptian and North African colloquial Arabic; the metaphor likely derives from the shared conceptual schema of a ring-like muscular opening; using this term for the anus is recognizable but not universal across dialects."}
